# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository and on checkout.
#
# Not a style preference. Editing on Windows rewrites whole files with CRLF,
# and git then reports every line of them as changed: one such edit produced a
# 7,965-line diff over 831 lines of actual work, which buries the change and
# destroys blame for every line it touches. eol=lf makes the working tree match
# the repository on every platform, so an editor that rewrites endings produces
# no diff at all.
#
# Everything here is text today. The binary declarations below are insurance:
# a font or an image added to web/ later must never be normalised, and a
# mistake in that direction corrupts the file silently.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts and the systemd unit are read by tools that reject CR.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
Makefile        text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf

# Assets: never touched.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.svg           text eol=lf
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary

# Model weights and audio are downloaded rather than committed, but declaring
# them costs nothing and a normalised .onnx or .wav is an unusable one.
*.onnx          binary
*.wav           binary
*.mp3           binary
*.opus          binary
*.m4a           binary
*.tar.gz        binary
*.tar.bz2       binary
*.zip           binary
